.TH KURUMIFRP 1 "November 2026" "KurumiFRP" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kurumifrp \- local workbench with an embedded frp runtime
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kurumifrp
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The native Linux package installs a systemd service running as the unprivileged
kurumifrp account. The management interface binds only to 127.0.0.1 on a free port
between 9000 and 9010. Configuration and runtime data reside under
/opt/kurumifrp/runtime. Existing web servers and firewall rules are not modified.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --show-token
Print the existing local management token. With system package state this requires
root or the dedicated service account. Do not publish this token.
.TP
.B --check-running
Verify that the installed manager responds to an authenticated local API request.
.TP
.B --stop
Request authenticated shutdown and wait for managed instances to stop.
.TP
.B --version
Print the application version.
.SH ADMINISTRATION
Use systemctl status/start/stop kurumifrp. Read the chosen address from
/opt/kurumifrp/runtime/data/listen.addr. Use SSH port forwarding for remote access.
The workbench starts at boot; only instances configured for automatic restore start.
The service has no capability to bind privileged ports below 1024.
.SH REMOVAL
Debian/Ubuntu: apt remove preserves state; apt purge kurumifrp removes application
state, keys, retained legacy cores and the service account created by this package.
RPM: final dnf remove kurumifrp removes managed state; upgrade preserves it.
System logs, browser records and user-created exports are not erased.
